WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.954
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level four.

2
00:00:06.254 --> 00:00:09.849
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:10.149 --> 00:00:17.550
Unit twenty-three: Pandemic Preparedness. Today you will explain earlier events with the past perfect continuous.

4
00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:20.526
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:21.467 --> 00:00:24.978
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:25.817 --> 00:00:26.771
Pandemic.

7
00:00:27.783 --> 00:00:31.640
Pandemic means a disease outbreak that spreads across many countries.

8
00:00:32.250 --> 00:00:34.911
The pandemic closed schools worldwide.

9
00:00:35.517 --> 00:00:37.914
Your turn. Say: Pandemic.

10
00:00:40.767 --> 00:00:41.733
Pandemic.

11
00:00:42.483 --> 00:00:43.531
Preparedness.

12
00:00:44.517 --> 00:00:48.026
Preparedness means being ready in advance to deal with a crisis.

13
00:00:48.617 --> 00:00:50.639
Preparedness saves lives and money.

14
00:00:51.250 --> 00:00:53.659
Your turn. Say: Preparedness.

15
00:00:56.500 --> 00:00:57.548
Preparedness.

16
00:00:58.283 --> 00:00:59.493
Surveillance.

17
00:01:00.483 --> 00:01:04.909
Surveillance means the careful tracking of illness to detect outbreaks early.

18
00:01:05.517 --> 00:01:08.015
Surveillance found the first cases quickly.

19
00:01:08.617 --> 00:01:11.629
Your turn. Say: Surveillance.

20
00:01:14.467 --> 00:01:15.804
Surveillance.

21
00:01:16.550 --> 00:01:17.505
Vaccine.

22
00:01:18.517 --> 00:01:22.014
Vaccine means a medicine that trains the body to fight a disease.

23
00:01:22.617 --> 00:01:25.708
The vaccine was tested on thousands of volunteers.

24
00:01:26.317 --> 00:01:28.818
Your turn. Say: Vaccine.

25
00:01:31.667 --> 00:01:33.097
Vaccine.

26
00:01:33.850 --> 00:01:35.165
Supply chain.

27
00:01:36.150 --> 00:01:40.425
Supply chain means the network that makes and delivers goods such as medicines.

28
00:01:41.017 --> 00:01:43.539
A damaged supply chain delayed the masks.

29
00:01:44.150 --> 00:01:47.046
Your turn. Say: Supply chain.

30
00:01:49.900 --> 00:01:51.110
Supply chain.

31
00:01:51.850 --> 00:01:53.037
Cooperation.

32
00:01:54.050 --> 00:01:57.547
Cooperation means countries and scientists working together.

33
00:01:58.150 --> 00:02:00.684
Cooperation sped up vaccine research.

34
00:02:01.283 --> 00:02:03.843
Your turn. Say: Cooperation.

35
00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:07.968
Cooperation.

36
00:02:08.717 --> 00:02:09.811
Outbreak.

37
00:02:10.817 --> 00:02:14.755
Outbreak means a sudden rise in cases of a disease in one area.

38
00:02:15.350 --> 00:02:17.593
The outbreak began in a busy market.

39
00:02:18.183 --> 00:02:20.685
Your turn. Say: Outbreak.

40
00:02:23.533 --> 00:02:24.558
Outbreak.

41
00:02:25.317 --> 00:02:26.318
Early warning.

42
00:02:27.317 --> 00:02:31.708
Early warning means a signal that danger is coming, given in time to act.

43
00:02:32.317 --> 00:02:35.141
An early warning system alerted hospitals.

44
00:02:35.750 --> 00:02:38.356
Your turn. Say: Early warning.

45
00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:42.329
Early warning.

46
00:02:43.133 --> 00:02:46.656
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:47.517 --> 00:02:55.219
Visal, a historian writes 'By the time the alarm was raised, the virus had been spreading for weeks.' What is 'had been spreading'?

48
00:02:56.317 --> 00:03:07.621
The past perfect continuous: had been + -ing. It shows an action that was in progress for some time before another past moment. Here the spreading started before the alarm.

49
00:03:08.717 --> 00:03:10.821
How is it different from the past perfect?

50
00:03:11.917 --> 00:03:26.182
Past perfect states completion: 'Scientists had identified the virus.' The continuous stresses duration or an unfinished process: 'Scientists had been tracking it for weeks.'

51
00:03:27.283 --> 00:03:29.271
Can you give an example with a cause?

52
00:03:30.383 --> 00:03:40.968
'Hospitals were exhausted because staff had been working without rest.' The continuous action explains the situation. Use for or since to show how long.

53
00:03:42.133 --> 00:03:43.529
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:44.383 --> 00:03:48.949
Grammar focus: Past perfect continuous: had been plus -ing.

55
00:03:49.249 --> 00:03:50.528
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:51.217 --> 00:03:55.550
By the time the alarm sounded, the virus had been spreading for several weeks.

57
00:03:56.450 --> 00:04:00.574
Hospitals were exhausted because staff had been working without rest since March.

58
00:04:01.483 --> 00:04:04.679
Scientists realised they had been tracking the wrong variant.

59
00:04:05.633 --> 00:04:13.426
Use had been + -ing for an action that continued up to a past moment and explains it: The virus had been spreading for weeks before the alarm.

60
00:04:14.183 --> 00:04:17.416
Be careful! Don't say: She had been wait for an hour.

61
00:04:19.367 --> 00:04:21.889
Say: She had been waiting for an hour.

62
00:04:22.189 --> 00:04:23.840
Use -ing after had been.

63
00:04:24.633 --> 00:04:27.320
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:28.133 --> 00:04:29.819
Lessons from a Pandemic.

65
00:04:30.119 --> 00:04:35.056
When the pandemic reached the news, the virus had already been spreading quietly for weeks.

66
00:04:36.067 --> 00:04:40.853
Weak surveillance meant that early warning signals had been ignored, and the outbreak grew.

67
00:04:41.867 --> 00:04:48.023
Hospitals struggled because staff had been working for months without rest, and the supply chain had been breaking down.

68
00:04:49.033 --> 00:04:54.249
A vaccine arrived faster than anyone had been expecting, thanks to scientific cooperation.

69
00:04:55.233 --> 00:05:02.051
The main lesson is preparedness: detect early, share data, protect supplies and trust expert advice.

70
00:05:03.167 --> 00:05:07.064
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:07.917 --> 00:05:11.379
By 2020, the virus blank for weeks.

72
00:05:11.679 --> 00:05:15.565
A, had been spreading. B, was spread. C, spreads.

73
00:05:19.100 --> 00:05:21.436
The answer is A: had been spreading.

74
00:05:22.583 --> 00:05:24.455
Which sentence stresses duration?

75
00:05:24.755 --> 00:05:29.779
A, They had left.. B, They had been waiting for hours.. C, They left..

76
00:05:33.333 --> 00:05:36.297
The answer is B: They had been waiting for hours..

77
00:05:37.450 --> 00:05:41.539
A signal that gives time to act before danger arrives is an early blank.

78
00:05:41.839 --> 00:05:44.982
A, warning. B, treaty. C, tariff.

79
00:05:48.533 --> 00:05:50.394
The answer is A: warning.

80
00:05:51.550 --> 00:05:54.908
The sudden spread of a disease in one area is an blank.

81
00:05:55.208 --> 00:05:59.291
A, outbreak. B, alliance. C, exchange.

82
00:06:02.833 --> 00:06:05.042
The answer is A: outbreak.

83
00:06:06.183 --> 00:06:08.845
According to the reading, what is the main lesson?

84
00:06:09.145 --> 00:06:12.914
A, preparedness. B, secrecy. C, delay.

85
00:06:16.467 --> 00:06:18.478
The answer is A: preparedness.

86
00:06:19.667 --> 00:06:22.691
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:23.550 --> 00:06:26.478
What lessons did the world learn from COVID-19?

88
00:06:35.283 --> 00:06:36.656
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:36.956 --> 00:07:00.860
The world learned that early warning, honest data sharing and strong health systems save lives. Countries that had invested in surveillance and supply chains responded faster. Vaccines were developed quickly through cooperation, but unequal access showed why fair distribution matters. The key lesson is to prepare before the next outbreak begins.

90
00:07:01.160 --> 00:07:02.730
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:03.617 --> 00:07:09.473
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and past perfect continuous: had been plus -ing.

92
00:07:09.773 --> 00:07:14.933
Today's value is resilience. Prepare for setbacks, and recover from them with strength.

93
00:07:15.233 --> 00:07:18.559
See you in Unit twenty-four. Goodbye!

94
00:07:18.859 --> 00:07:21.767
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

